---
title: 'AI Toolkit'
enterprise: true
---

A collection of API methods and parameters that make integrating LLMs with AG Grid straightforward.

By using the AI Toolkit you can allow users to interact with the grid using natural language. The example below demonstrates how you might provide a chat interface to your users, allowing them to ask questions about the data in the grid and have the grid respond with the appropriate state changes.

Here are a few you can try:

- "Show me all the gold medals won by the USA"
- "Sort the competitors with the youngest first"
- "Group by country and show the total number of medals won"

{% gridExampleRunner title="Natural Language Grid State Management" name="natural-language-grid-state" /%}

## Structured Schema

To allow you to keep control over what aspects of the grid your users can manipulate using natural language, the grid exposes the API method `getStructuredSchema`, which returns a [JSON schema](https://json-schema.org/) that can be sent to an LLM as part of a prompt.

Many LLMs, including [ChatGPT](https://platform.openai.com/docs/guides/structured-outputs) and [Gemini](https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/structured-output), support the ability to return structured data that conforms to a provided schema. By providing the schema returned by `getStructuredSchema` to the LLM you can ensure that the response from the LLM can be passed directly to the `setState` API method.

If you wish to integrate with an LLM that does not support structured data natively, you can still use the schema to validate and parse the response from the LLM before passing it to `setState`. There are multiple libraries that can do this for you, such as [ajv](https://ajv.js.org/).

The schema returned by `getStructuredSchema` is a narrow representation of what can be achieved using the grid's API. For example, if a column is not sortable, the schema will not include that column in the list of sortable columns. This ensures that the LLM is only able to generate valid state changes for the grid.

### Features

The schema is made up of a series of "features", each representing a different aspect of the grid that can be manipulated. The following features are currently supported:
- Filtering
- Sorting
- Aggregation
- Pivoting
- Row Grouping
- Column Visibility
- Column Sizing

By default, all features are enabled and included in the schema. If you wish to limit the features returned in the schema, you can do so by providing a list of feature names to the `getStructuredSchema` method.

For example, if you only want to allow users to manipulate column visibility and sorting, you can call `getStructuredSchema` like this:
```ts
const schema = gridApi.getStructuredSchema({ exclude: ['columnVisibility', 'sorting'] });
```

Since `setState` will by default overwrite all properties of the grid state, you should make sure to use the secondary parameter in setGridState which allows you you to specify features to ignore in the change. You can even ask your LLM to include a list of features it hasn't changed, and pass that list directly to `setState`. Alternatively you may wish to merge the current state with the new state before passing it to `setState`. The example above asks the LLM to provide a set of features which should be ignored by `setState` so it doesn't overwrite other settings.

### Options

Occasionally the LLM will need more information than what can be provided by the grid alone. As such, you can pass in options for each column defining extra context.

 - `description` - This provides a description of what the column contains to the LLM inline in the schema. This might include details such as the type or format of the data, to aid it when filtering or aggregating.
 - `includeSetValues` - When using the Set Filter, the LLM must be provided with the allowed values for it to correctly set those it wishes to filter. However, some Set Filters contain many values which lead to a schema which is too large for your LLM to process. By default we do not include the Set Filter values, however if you set this property to true then they will be included.

```ts
const schema = gridApi.getStructuredSchema({ columns: {
    sport: {
        description: "The sport the athlete won their medal in",
        includeSetValues: true
    },
    gold: {
        description: "The number of gold medals won by this athlete at this games"
    }
}});
```

